"Reduced normal form of the zero-sum game of Figure 3(a). Player 1 plays D in every Nash equilibrium, so the subgame perfect equilibrium in which player 2 then plays d if reached relies on player 2 believing that player 1 would play delta rather than alpha. The unique stable set contains both (D,d) and (D, a mixture of d and a) for player 2, so a stable set can contain more outcomes than survive iterated elimination of dominated strategies.

Player 1's strategies are D, and delta or alpha at the second node after A and a; the labels are the paper's."

